<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jablonec nad Nisou</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gablonz an der Neisse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jablonec nad Nisou, in German Gablonz an der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a>, is a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, famous since the 18th century for glass jewellery and costume ornaments. Its largely German population was expelled after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and many of the refugees resettled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neugablonz">Neugablonz</a> near Kaufbeuren in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, where they rebuilt the industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jablonec-nad-nisou.jpg" alt="Jablonec nad Nisou" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miloslav Rejha · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jablonec_nad_Nisou_2019-07_16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jablonec nad Nisou lies about 7 km south-east of Liberec and 83 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, at the confluence of the Lusatian Neisse and the Bílá Nisa in the eastern tip of the Zittau Basin, ringed by the Jizera Mountains. The Czech name means little apple tree, because the village was founded where an apple tree grew. German-speaking settlers of the 16th century turned it into Gablonz, and in 1904 the suffix nad Nisou, or an der Neisse, was made official in both languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first written mention is in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> document of 1356, which treats the village as founded in the 14th century. Troops of rebelling Lusatians burned it in August 1469 during their war with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-podebrady">George of Poděbrady</a>, and it was resettled in the 1530s and 1540s by mostly German-speaking colonists. In the first half of the 17th century it belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a>, who sold it to the Desfours family, and it was burned again in 1643 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>.</p><p>Glass production began to flourish in the second half of the 17th century. The first bijou was made in the 18th century, and the exporter J. F. Schwann spread the village's name through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Emperor Francis II made it a market town on 21 April 1808, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a> made it a town on 28 March 1866. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> of 1870 to 1871 damaged competitors, its traders took over foreign markets. By the turn of the 20th century Bohemian garnet jewellery sold in Chicago came from Gablonz. The crash of 1929 hit the glass and jewellery trade, and foreign competition and changing fashion added to the crisis of the 1930s.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> the town was annexed to Nazi Germany in October 1938 and administered as part of Reichsgau Sudetenland until 1945. Between 1945 and 1949 most of the Germans were expelled under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benes-decrees">Beneš decrees</a>. Some of them founded a new settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and named it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neugablonz">Neugablonz</a>, new Jablonec. It is now a district of Kaufbeuren.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jablonec nad Nisou is a city of about 46,000 inhabitants, the second largest in the Liberec Region, and it forms a conurbation with Liberec. Its historic centre is protected as an urban monument zone. The New Town Hall, built in the functionalist style in 1930 to 1933, is a national cultural monument. The Church of the Exaltation of the Holy Cross of 1900 to 1902 is counted among the most important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a> churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The Museum of Glass and Costume Jewellery, founded in 1904, presents the local tradition of costume jewellery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jablonec nad Nisou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Second World War German refugees from <b>Gablonz</b> in Bohemia settled its suburb of Neugablonz, bringing their glass and jewellery industry.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-kaufbeuren">Free Imperial City of Kaufbeuren</a></li></ul></section>
